{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:inflation:reconciler","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:9dc1f6983c9934ccf3e38333d351fd42073871c860bdf9b39712762036f4be6e","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.12,"B":0.57,"C":0.17,"D":0.14}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"INPUTS, read off MY OWN answers, not off any panelist's. Growth, from my CQ3 (P10 0.90 / P50 1.75 / P90 2.75, sd ~0.72): P(growth >= 2.6) = 0.12; P(growth < 2.0) = 0.64; P(growth < 1.7) = 0.47. Core for the LOCK'S WINDOW, which is 2032-2036 only and NOT CQ2's 2027-2036: my CQ2 path decomposes into 2027-2030 at ~2.95 and 2031-2036 at ~2.45, so the 2032-2036 segment central is ~2.40 on the GDP-weighted CPI-style reading; blending in the 30% core-PCE/consumption-weighted branch at ~2.20 gives an expected central of 2.35, sd 0.48. This central ALREADY contains the ~+0.30 pp EM-OECD floor (Turkey ~+0.27 pp of it). Hence P(core_32-36 >= 2.70) = 1 - Phi(0.729) = 0.233; P(core_32-36 < 2.00) = Phi(-0.729) = 0.233; P(2.00-2.70) = 0.534.\n\nLEXICAL APPLICATION, FIRST MATCH WINS.\nStep 1, A = P(growth >= 2.6) = 0.12 -> 12.\nStep 2, C = P(core >= 2.70) x P(growth < 2.0 | core >= 2.70) = 0.233 x 0.72 = 0.168 -> 17. The conditional is lifted from the 0.64 unconditional because a high back-half core at low growth must be substantially supply-side in my world — the care bottleneck clearing partly through prices, a grid wall surviving capex digestion on trades scarcity, or fiscal dominance — but it is well below the Outside-View's 0.68-equivalent and below what a pure stagflation narrative would take, because part of my high-core mass is strong-nominal-demand and growth-neutral.\nStep 3, D = P(core < 2.00) x P(growth < 1.7 | core < 2.00) = 0.233 x 0.62 = 0.144 -> 14. Lifted from 0.47 for the demand-shortfall correlation (post-capex-bust excess capacity and the BIS Box C labour-income deficit produce both legs).\nStep 4, B = 100 - 12 - 17 - 14 = 57. Sums to exactly 100.\n\nRESIDUAL-B DECOMPOSITION, so B is an accounting residual and not a muddle-through judgement: P(1.7 <= g < 2.6) = 1 - 0.47 - 0.12 = 0.41, less the slice C takes in that band with core >= 2.7 (~0.04) = 0.37; plus the low-growth / moderate-core leftover cell at g < 1.7 with core in [2.00, 2.70) = 0.47 - 0.14 (D) - 0.11 (C's g < 1.7 share) = 0.22. Total 0.59, matching the residual 0.57 to within rounding. So only ~37 of my 57 is the literal 1.7-2.5% barbell; ~20 is the leftover cell a naive reader would have assigned to D.\n\nCONSISTENCY CHECK AGAINST MY OWN CQ3. A = 12 is numerically identical to P(growth >= 2.6) read off my CQ3 distribution. It is not a judgement about the appeal of a productivity renaissance and it is NOT the historical base rate, which puts ~85-90% of 1960-2019 five-year windows for this aggregate above 2.6% — the Outside-View panelist's single most important finding was that this base rate must be overridden because A's threshold is arithmetically out of reach once the near-deterministic headcount is imposed (it needs China at ~4.5%+ AND OECD at ~1.6%+ simultaneously). My CQ3 P50 of 1.75 sits inside B's literal band, and B is modal at 57 — consistent. I reject the Red-Team's A = 20, which comes entirely from his China leg at 3.3% (measurement wedge plus political floor, partly double-counted) and which he himself conceded includes ~5 points of a nominal-reflation cell that fails A's own label clauses on broad wage gains and growth-stabilized debt.\n\nCONSISTENCY CHECK AGAINST MY OWN CQ2. CQ2 = 38 is a JOINT: OECD core >= 2.50 over 2027-2036 at 0.58, times the China leg at 0.68 with rho -0.05. The OECD leg's 0.58 must NOT be mapped onto C, which needs core >= 2.70 over 2032-2036 only, marginal 0.233. The two are consistent ONLY because my path is front-loaded (2.95 then 2.45), and that front-loading is the single assumption a reader should check me on — on a flat path the two numbers would conflict and C would have to rise ~10-14 points. This window mismatch was the Resolution Auditor's best finding and it survived Wave 2 untouched; it is worth roughly 12-15 points of CQ6 mass that a naive reader puts in C.\n\nWHERE I LAND BETWEEN THE PANEL. C at 17 sits between Wave 1's 11-18 and the Red-Team's 25: I accept his core-goods rigidity and half his care-price politics, which raise the core marginal from Fermi's 0.19 to 0.233, but not his anchor drift, which is what would take C to 25. D at 14 is my largest single departure from the Inside-View's 34, and for two stated reasons rather than one. First, the Auditor's audited EM-OECD floor means D requires G7 core at ~1.65-1.70, a deeper Japanification than the narrative pictures — an index-construction constraint, not a macro view, worth ~6 points out of D. Second, the Red-Team landed a real hit on an internal contradiction: the Inside-View derives his disinflationary back half from turbines, transformers and datacentre shells arriving into stalled load, which requires the capacity to have been BUILT, while his own coupling section argues buildable load is -18 to -25% because the trades are drained by aging and bid for by housing and the care-facility build. You cannot have both excess electrical capacity and a labour-constrained under-build. Resolving toward under-build makes the back half energy- and shelter-inflationary while still growth-poor, moving mass from D toward C and B. I price that at ~8-10 points rather than his 15.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:52.925Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Turkish annual CPI, as published by TurkStat, averages below 7.0% over calendar 2027-2029 (arithmetic mean of the three December-on-December prints), with Mexico, Chile and Colombia each averaging below 3.5% over the same three years.\",\"by_when\":\"Jan 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The cleanest single falsifier of MY numbers, because I have made this term the largest and most mechanical in the payload. It removes the ~+0.30 pp EM-OECD floor: my GDP-weighted OECD-38 core central falls from ~2.65 to ~2.45 and P(core >= 2.50 over 2027-2036) drops from 0.58 to ~0.38, taking CQ2 from 38 to ~26. The 2032-2036 central falls from 2.35 to ~2.18, cutting CQ6-C from 17 to ~11 and raising CQ6-D from 14 to ~20, with B roughly unchanged at ~57. CQ4 P50 falls ~15 bp.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US CPI core commodities (core goods) 12-month rate at or below 0.0% for calendar-year 2029, while Section 301/232-style tariff coverage remains in force at or above 2026 breadth.\",\"by_when\":\"Jan 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the one Red-Team hit I partially adopted — downward nominal rigidity converting the relative-price skew into the level. My core-goods term reverts from 0.8% to the Wave-1 plug of ~0.4% (-0.09 pp on the US leg, ~-0.07 pp on the aggregate), and with it the whole claim that tariffs and fragmentation have changed the regime in sectors that 'want' to deflate. CQ2 falls from 38 to ~32; CQ6-C from 17 to ~13 with the mass to B; CQ5 conjunct A falls ~0.03, taking CQ5 from 27 to ~25. It would also mean the Red-Team contrarian-posed on his single largest departure from Wave 1 and I should not have moved at all.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI (NBS monthly series, any basket vintage) prints 2.0% year-on-year or higher for four consecutive quarters at any point between 2027 and 2031, with the non-food, non-residence component contributing at least 1.0 pp of it (i.e. not a pork or energy spike).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills CQ2's stronger conjunct and the China-as-semi-independent-deflationary-object premise my effort brief required me to carry. P(China CPI < 1.50% over 2027-2036) falls from 0.68 to ~0.38 and CQ2 drops from 38 to ~22 even with my OECD leg fully intact — which is the cleanest available test of whether my CQ2 comes from the OECD leg (my claim) or from the correlation assumption. It also lifts my China real leg from 3.05% toward 3.4%, raising CQ3 P50 from 1.75 to ~1.88 and shifting ~4 points of CQ6 mass out of D into B and C.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Cumulative publicly disclosed AI-related capex impairments and cancellations stay below 10% of announced hyperscaler buildouts AND net external AI software/API revenue exceeds 1.0x annualized AI compute D&A for four consecutive quarters, with at least 70 GW of net firm capacity serving data centres reaching commercial operation across OECD-38 + China (EIA / ENTSO-E / CEC commissioning data) by end-2031.\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The highest-leverage signpost for my payload, because it removes the locked AI-Q4 bust from the middle of every 2032-2036 measurement window and simultaneously refutes my coupled claim that trades labour and interconnection bind harder than the dollars. Capital-services growth stays at ~1.8%/yr (+0.30 pp on the OECD leg) and the AI savings-demand term returns to +0.25 pp on r*. CQ3 P50 moves from 1.75 to ~2.05 and P90 from 2.75 to ~3.00; CQ4 P50 from 2.00 to ~2.30 with a thinner left tail; CQ6-A from 12 to ~19, D from 14 to ~9, B to ~57, C ~15; CQ5 falls from 27 to ~21 as the back-half MOVE path drops toward 85 and the leveraged-unwind shock source thins. It would also retire my AI-Q3 coupling correction.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An equity drawdown of >= 20% (MSCI World, USD, closing basis) occurs in which the G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal sovereign yield FALLS by >= 100 bp between the equity peak date and the trough date — the classic flight-to-quality reasserting itself despite convenience-yield erosion, basis-trade absorption and >100% debt/GDP.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the fiscal-dominance correlation structure behind both CQ5 and CQ4's right tail. P(no 50 bp rally | drawdown) falls from 0.45 to ~0.28 and the A|B conditional from 0.65 to ~0.52, taking CQ5 from 27 to ~16. CQ4 P90 falls from 3.35 to ~2.95 and the distribution loses its right skew. ~3 points of CQ6 mass move from C to D, because bond-market tolerance means repression is unnecessary and accommodation of inflation is less likely.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 3 reconciler seat, effort 'inflation'."}}